# File Locations

Place a module next to the single page or layout that uses it. Promote it to
`src/` only when a second consumer appears.

## Page- or layout-local

If a component or helper is used by only one `page.tsx` or `layout.tsx`,
colocate it in a sibling folder of that entrypoint:

- Components: `_components/<name>/`
- Helpers: `_helpers/`

```
src/app/blog/[category]/[slug]/
  page.tsx
  _helpers/resolve-article.ts
  _components/render/render.tsx
  _components/back-to-top/back-to-top.tsx
```

Import with a relative path from the entrypoint:

```tsx
import { Render } from "./_components/render/render";
import { BackToTop } from "./_components/back-to-top/back-to-top";
```

Do not put single-use modules in `src/components` or `src/lib`.

## Shared

If a component or helper is used from more than one page, layout, or other
module, put it in a named folder:

- `src/components/<name>/`
- `src/lib/<name>.ts` or `src/lib/<name>/`

`BlogCard` is used by blog grids and archives, so it lives in
`src/components/blog-card/`.

When a second consumer appears, move the module from `_components` or
`_helpers` into `src/components` or `src/lib` and update imports.

Storyblok CMS mapping components are shared by the renderer and live in
`src/bloks/`. Keep UI markup in `src/components`; bloks only adapt blok data.

A CMS story renderer used by more than one route may stay under
`src/app/[...slug]/_components/render/` and be imported via the `@/` alias.

## Internal module boundaries

Keep entry modules focused on their declared role:

- `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, and route handlers contain framework exports only.
- `"use server"` action modules export action functions only.
- Feature-private pure utilities belong in a sibling `_helpers/` directory.
- Feature-private schemas and validators belong in a sibling `_schema/`
  directory.

Promote pure utilities and schemas to a named `src/lib/` module when they are
imported across feature boundaries. Do not import shared utilities from a
component or proxy-plugin implementation directory.

Keep functions in place when they are inseparable implementation details of a
single component, hook, data adapter, or parser. Do not create a separate file
for every small expression.

## Tests

Colocate unit tests with the source file they test, following the unit-tests
rule: `thing.ts` → `thing.spec.ts` in the same directory.
